{"title":"Perfect number","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>A <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive_integer\" title=\"Positive integer\">positive integer</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is called a <b>perfect number</b> if it is the sum of its <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Proper_divisor\" title=\"Proper divisor\">proper divisors</a>.\n</p><p>The first four perfect numbers are:\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/d/e3dc029749cc5d357b3fa31b0ae91876d001f26d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 = 3 + 2 + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"104\" height=\"14\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/4/3f4a72241914725a883eb56f655105efa44569ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$28 = 14 + 7 + 4 + 2 + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"185\" height=\"14\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/6/b36f57ebff398fc425f7a513277db241fea87b0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$496 = 248 + 124 + 62 + 31 + 16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"373\" height=\"14\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/1/c01a1cb1852b45959c5763b070f35c32031a4494.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8128 = 4064 + 2032 + 1016 + 508 + 254 + 127 + 64 + 32 + 16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"608\" height=\"14\" /></li></ul>\n<p>These were the only perfect numbers known to ancient mathematicians.  \nSome other perfect numbers include:\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/1/991b0ca853fb303513f927755726364d6e97943f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$33550336$\" width=\"71\" height=\"12\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/7/f777451088426402243a2c5a26b2730d654dce98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8589869056$\" width=\"90\" height=\"12\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/a/06a60b7216bc3a87ede28953bda03bb7baba0632.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$137438691328$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"108\" height=\"13\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/f/99f639577b0bac5242d3e80f82547611e5729537.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2305843008139952128$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"171\" height=\"13\" /></li></ul>\n<p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Even_Perfect_Numbers\">Even Perfect Numbers</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Perfect_number&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Even Perfect Numbers\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Even_integer\" title=\"Even integer\"> even</a> perfect number if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/0/080141928c19a1f79fd7fa1794d4d0d0df841e6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=\\frac{p(p+1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"101\" height=\"38\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_number\" title=\"Prime number\">prime number</a> of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/f/f0f53783d5dad75348400c0088fe1c38e89c9d9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"47\" height=\"16\" /> for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />.\nPrimes of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/f/f0f53783d5dad75348400c0088fe1c38e89c9d9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"47\" height=\"16\" /> are called <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mersenne_prime\" title=\"Mersenne prime\">Mersenne primes</a>. \n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Perfect_number&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>It is conjectured that there are are infinitely many Mersenne primes and hence infinitely many even perfect numbers. No <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Odd_integer\" title=\"Odd integer\"> odd</a> perfect numbers are known, and any that do exist must be greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/9/bd95c85b17539071e291175cb1db57c2be681a49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^{500}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" />. It is conjectured that there are none. No one has been able to prove or disprove these conjectures.\n</p><p><br />\nNumbers which are not perfect may be either <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Deficient_number\" title=\"Deficient number\">deficient numbers</a> or <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Abundant_number\" title=\"Abundant number\">abundant numbers</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Perfect_number&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"http://mathforum.org/dr.math/faq/faq.perfect.html\">http://mathforum.org/dr.math/faq/faq.perfect.html</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 2026101